  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>On the Promenade des Anglais, right opposite the Lenval hospital, a stretch of beach is reserved for dogs. It's easy to spot: in summer it is marked by two statues of Totor, a surfing and lifeguard dog, by the artist Stéphane Bolongaro, and by barriers. It's one of Nice's two dog beaches, alongside La Lanterne, further west.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>According to the City of Nice (municipal orders 2008-00481 and 2010-2049, article 26), this beach is open to dogs year-round. The tourist office specifies the rule by category: category 3 dogs may be off-leash and swim, category 2 dogs must be muzzled and kept on a leash, and category 1 dogs are banned.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: check your dog's category before coming. In summer, avoid the hottest hours: the pebbles get very hot, common-sense advice rather than an official rule.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The Promenade des Anglais is great for a long walk before or after the swim, and the Old Town of Nice with its lively alleyways is just a few minutes' walk away.</p>
